As demonstrated in the table above, there are four case types in Tuparí: nuclear, locative, instrumental-lative, and oblique. Additionally, under certain conditions, Nuclear and Locative cases are able to stack (for reasons that will be discussed below). As mentioned by Singerman (2018), the case markings are used in complementary distribution, unless they are referring to time, in which case LOC (on Portuguese loan-words), INS (on Tuparí temporal expressions) and OBL (in finite embedded clauses marking time) may be used.

==== Nuclear case ====
As discussed by Singerman (2018, section 2.4),{{sfn|Singerman|2018}}{{rp|62}} the nuclear case is subject to strict grammatical constraints. It is required on all NP subjects, as well as on strong pronouns that introduce a new topic.

An example of NUC case-marking on an NP subject:

{{interlinear |lang=tpr |indent=2 |glossing3=yes
|Eowet ke tewakto pete’a.
|e-op-et ke te-wak-to pete’a
|2SG-father-NUC like.this 3C-cry-NMZ<sub>''ro''</sub> FUT.3SG+TH
|‘Your father will cry like this.’ {{sfn|Singerman|2018}}{{rp|64}}
}}

NUC case marking is optional for non-pronominal direct objects, and is barred from appearing on clause-initial foci and incorporated direct objects. The three cases in which nonpronominal direct objects do not bear a NUC case-marking are as follows: direct objects that have not previously been mentioned in the discourse, genitive possessors (never marked) and nominal predicates. Regarding direct objects, while the first mention of the object in the discourse will be bare of case-marking, subsequent mentions of the same object will bear case marking. Direct objects will also tend to not be case-marked when following a negation or negative element in the sentence. Weak nominative enclitics do not bear any case morphology.

Incorporated objects do not bear Nuclear Case:

{{interlinear |lang=tpr |indent=2 |glossing3=yes
|Opẽan’aropmã ko ’on.
|o-pẽan-arop-mã-a ko ’on
|1SG-first-food-put-TH POLITE.FUT 1SG
|‘Let me put my food [on my plate] first.’ {{sfn|Singerman|2018}}{{rp|67}}
}}

===== Possessors =====
Possessors in Tuparí are always unmarked for case, thus demonstrating that the language has no overt [[Genitive case|Genitive]] case. To account for case never being marked for incorporated direct objects and clause-initial foci, and sometimes being marked for unincorporated direct objects, it has been posited by Fery and Isihara (2016)<ref>{{Cite book|title=The Oxford Handbook of Information Structure|last=Féry|first=Caroline|publisher=Oxford University Press|year=2016|isbn=978-0-19-964267-0}}</ref> and Krifka and Musan (2012),<ref>{{Cite book|title=The Expression of Information Structure|last1=Krifka|first1=Manfred|last2=Musan|publisher=De Gruyter Mouton|year=2012}}</ref> among others, that Tuparí has a givenness or topicality condition, notably that case marking does not appear when the object is introduced, but does appear on later mentions of that object throughout the discourse. This accounts for the ban of suffixes on [[Focus (linguistics)|focal clause]]-initial NPs, as well as the variability as to whether unincorporated objects will bear case marking. This also highlights that nuclear case is sensitive to information structural considerations, due to these notions of [[Focus (linguistics)|focus]], givenness and [[Topic (linguistics)|topicality]].

An example of the lack of Nuclear case on Focal NPs:

{{interlinear |lang=tpr |number=a. |glossing3=yes
|Òwet Tupari.
|o-op-et Tupari(*-t)
|1SG-father-NUC Tuparí(*-NUC)
|‘My father is Tuparí.’ {{sfn|Singerman|2018}}{{rp|69}}
}}

{{interlinear |lang=tpr |number=b. |glossing3=yes
|Òpbe {} Tuparit.
|o-op(*-et) e Tupari-t
|1SG-father(*-NUC) 3 Tuparí-NUC
|‘It is my father who is Tuparí.’ / ‘My father is the one who is Tuparí.’ {{sfn|Singerman|2018}}{{rp|69}}
}}

==== Locative case ====
The locative case-marker {{lang|tpr|-pe}} is used to mark location, particularly when it is inside a structure or object.{{sfn|Singerman|2018}}{{rp|71}} It can be used in time expressions when combined with Portuguese loanwords depicting time, but is not seen in combination with time expressions in Tuparí. The {{lang|tpr|-pe}} morpheme can also attach to VPs to change the meaning of the verb to mean “after doing X”.

example of Locative {{lang|tpr|–pe}}:

{{interlinear |lang=tpr |indent=2 |glossing3=yes
|Het’aere nẽ èkpe kiret haytoe? {}
|het’aere nẽ e-ek-pe kire-t hayto e
|where.you.are {{gcl|Y/N|yes/no interrogative particle}} 2SG-house-LOC person-NUC a.lot 3
|‘Are there a lot of people where you are, in your house?’ {{sfn|Singerman|2018}}{{rp|71}}
}}

==== Instrumental – locative case ====
The instrumental-lative case marker has two meanings, notably that it demonstrates the instrument used to perform an action, or it can highlight a person or object that is physically involved in an action. Additionally, this morpheme can express the direction in which movement happens. Singerman (2018) notes that this INS morpheme is also used in Tuparí when expressing languages, since they are considered an instrument of communication.

{{interlinear |lang=tpr |indent=2 |glossing3=yes
|Kat’aro ’en èurap?
|kat’at-o ’en e-eut-ap
|what-INS 2SG 2SG-get.full-{{gcl|ADV.FOC|adverbial focus}}
|‘What did you fill up on?’ {{sfn|Singerman|2018}}{{rp|73}}
}}

==== Oblique case ====
The final case-marker in Tuparí is {{lang|tpr|-ere/ -re}}, which is used to mark oblique (OBL) case. Oblique case different from Locative case in that it marks the location of a place, or the movement from a place. It can also be used in certain temporal relations, as previously mentioned. Many intransitive verbs in Tuparí can take optional complements that bear oblique case-marking. This case-marker is often also seen stacked on top of the deverbalizing nominalizer {{lang|tpr|-ap}}.

{{interlinear |lang=tpr |indent=2 |glossing3=yes
|Here Koloradore otero’apbi’a ’on Pedro yare.
|here Kolorado-re o-tero’a-pbi’a ’on Pedro yare
|then Colorado-OBL 1SG-AUX<sub>go</sub>.SG.TH-NMZ<sub>''ap''</sub>-DUR 1SG Pedro alongside/with
|‘Back then, I was living in Colorado, with Pedro.’ {{sfn|Singerman|2018}}{{rp|77}}
}}

==== Nuclear + locative case stacking ====
A feature of the syntax of Tuparí is that case can be stacked in certain contexts, meaning that two case features can be overtly recognized on a single nominal base. On certain NPs (rightperipheral) the Locative {{lang|tpr|-pe}} case morpheme can be stacked onto the Nuclear case marker {{lang|tpr|-et/ -t}}. This is part of a broader tendency in Tuparí to build “new” cases by working with the four preexisting ones. Caspar and Rodrigues (1957)<ref>{{Cite book|title=Tuparí Grammar|last1=Caspar|first1=Franz|last2=Rodrigues|publisher="Versuch einer Grammatik der Tupari-Sprache.|year=1957}}</ref> and Alves (2004)<ref name=":5" /> have referred to the combination of Nuclear and Locative cases in Tuparí as a form of Accusative case, however Singerman (2018) challenges this, saying that the combination should rather be seen as a method to assert coreference in the discourse.

{{interlinear |lang=tpr |indent=2 |glossing3=yes
|Iyma’ẽka ko ’on hètpe!
|i-yma’ẽk-a ko ’on hè-t-pe
|3-speak.with-TH POLITE.FUT 1SG that.thing-NUC-LOC
|‘I want to talk to him, to that one!’ {{sfn|Singerman|2018}}{{rp|81}}
}}

== Semantics ==
Singerman 2018 says that “there is little to no quantification within noun phrases”. There is little adjectival modification, and nouns do not take any articles to mark definiteness.{{sfn|Singerman|2018}}{{rp|23}} Instead, quantification is affixed onto the verb in the form of two morphemes {{lang|tpr|erote-}} and {{lang|tpr|urut-}}, meaning ‘All, entirely’ and ‘two, both’ respectively. Note that {{lang|tpr|erote-}} changes to {{lang|tpr|irote-}} after 3rd person proclitic {{lang|tpr|s-}}.

{{interlinear |lang=tpr |indent=2 |glossing3=yes
|Amẽkòt kiparorot erotetãramka nã tero’a, saraerem e’awa tero’a te’a.
|Amẽko-t ki-paroro-t erote-tãramka-a nã tero’a, saraerem e’awa-a tero’e-a te-’a
|jaguar-NUC 1PL.INCL-armadillo-NUC all-kill.PL-TH PROG AUX<sub>go</sub>.SG.TH everyday hunt-TH AUX<sub>go</sub>.SG-TH 3C-when.SG
|‘The Jaguar is killing all of our armadillos when it’s out hunting everyday.’ {{sfn|Singerman|2018}}{{rp|144}}
}}

As seen above, when {{lang|tpr|erote-}} combines with a direct object, the direct object NP must have nuclear case. Definiteness/specificity seems to come from the presence of the nuclear case on the direct object, which allows {{lang|tpr|erote-}} ‘all’, which is generally only compatible with definite/specific objects. Singerman's consultants would not accept {{lang|tpr|erote-}} combining with any caseless objects.

{{interlinear |lang=tpr |indent=2 |glossing3=yes
|Korakorat terote’etãramka.
|korakora-t te-erote-e-tãramka-a
|chicken-NUC 3C-all-INTRNS-kill.PL-TH
|‘The chickens have all died.’ {{sfn|Singerman|2018}}{{rp|145}}
}}

{{interlinear |lang=tpr |indent=2 |glossing3=yes
|Werotepuop’orap kot’oa nã otero’e, wat’ema’erẽ.
|w-erote-puop’ot-ap kot’oy-a nã o-tero’e wat-ema’ẽ-re
|1SG-all-learn-NMZ<sub>''ap''</sub> want-TH PROG 1SG-AUX<sub>go</sub>.SG 2PL-language-OBL
|‘I am wanting to learn all of your-{{sc|PL}} words/all of your-{{sc|PL}} languages.’ {{sfn|Singerman|2018}}{{rp|145}}
}}

Singerman explains that the first person singular is not compatible with an ‘all’ reading, which is why {{lang|tpr|erote-}} instead quantifies ‘your languages-OBL’. These two examples show that it is possible for the {{lang|tpr|erote-}} to quantify either intransitive subjects or an oblique NP, however there is a gap in the data for what happens if both the intransitive subject and oblique object are compatible with {{lang|tpr|erote-}}.

The previously mentioned {{lang|tpr|urut-}} has been confirmed to exist in elicitation, however there are no examples from everyday conversation or text. It is likely derived from the noun {{lang|tpr|huru}} 'pair' but beyond that no data or analysis is offered. No other forms of quantification are discussed by Singerman.

